# Akamai API Security Lab Environment

Repeatable lab environment for testing and learning Akamai API Security (formerly Noname Security). Provisions AWS infrastructure via Terraform and configures it via Ansible. Kong OSS and NGINX OSS (OpenResty) run with Noname sensor plugins baked into custom ECR images. Anypoint Flex Gateway runs in connected mode, fronting a Juice Shop proxy API. A Noname Sensor DaemonSet runs on every cluster EC2 node and sniffs plaintext HTTP off the host NIC — that is the path the lab uses for Flex Gateway visibility, since the Mulesoft custom-policy is a Mule 4 Runtime policy and Anypoint refuses to apply it to Flex-served APIs. All three traffic sources (`lab-kong`, `lab-nginx`, `lab-aws-ecs`) register with the engine end-to-end.

**Target environment**: AWS us-east-2, single VPC, ECS with EC2 launch type (t3.medium — t3.small does not fit the sensor alongside the gateway tasks), local Terraform state.

## Common Commands

```bash
# First-time setup
make install-terraform   # installs Terraform to /usr/local/bin
make setup               # creates .venv, installs Ansible + Galaxy collections
make keys                # generates keys/lab_key (ED25519 SSH keypair)

# Copy and fill in secrets
cp terraform/terraform.tfvars.example terraform/terraform.tfvars
make vault-create        # creates and encrypts ansible/vault.yml from example

# Full deployment
make deploy              # terraform init -> apply -> ansible provision

# Individual steps
make init                # terraform init
make plan                # terraform plan
make apply               # terraform apply
make provision           # ansible-playbook site.yml

# Teardown
make destroy             # terraform destroy

# Linting
make lint                # runs both terraform and ansible linters
make lint-tf             # terraform fmt -check + validate + tflint
make lint-ansible        # ansible-lint

# Vault management
make vault-edit          # edit encrypted vault.yml

# Traffic generation (feeds Noname engine for behavioral learning)
make traffic-install     # install Locust into .venv (once after setup)
make traffic             # headless traffic across all 5 user classes, Ctrl+C to stop
make traffic-ui          # Locust web UI at http://localhost:8089
TRAFFIC_USERS=25 TRAFFIC_RATE=5 make traffic  # tune rate
USER_POOL_SIZE=2000 USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_VAMPI=200 USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_CRAPI=400 USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_JUICESHOP=200 IDENTITY_ROTATION_INTERVAL=5 make traffic  # per-class seed defaults shown — VAmPI capped <1 min, JuiceShop ~3 min, CrAPI ~8 min at TRAFFIC_USERS=50
make traffic-owasp       # OWASP API Top 10 attacks across all 5 apps; run AFTER ~30 min of 'make traffic' so attacks land as anomalies vs the baseline (do not run concurrently — baseline gets poisoned)
make traffic-owasp-ui    # Locust web UI for the attack file
ATTACK_USERS=10 ATTACK_RATE=2 make traffic-owasp  # tune attack cadence (intentionally lower than baseline — demo, not flood)

# Noname sensor plugin installation (run once per lab, requires Docker + integration-files/*.zip)
make deploy-plugins      # full flow: ECR -> build images -> apply -> provision -> plugin config
make plugin-images       # create ECR repos, build+push images, write terraform/plugin.auto.tfvars
make provision-plugins   # push Noname plugin config to Kong; verify NGINX health
```

All `make` targets are self-documented: run `make` (no args) to list them.

The Ansible vault password file is expected at `~/.vault_pass`. The `AWS_PROFILE` environment variable controls which AWS CLI profile Ansible uses for dynamic inventory.

## Repository Structure

```
terraform/
  main.tf                  # Root module — wires all child modules together
  variables.tf / outputs.tf
  terraform.tfvars.example # Copy to terraform.tfvars and fill in admin_cidr + Noname source values
  plugin.auto.tfvars       # Written by 'make plugin-images' (gitignored) — ECR URIs + plugin flag
  modules/
    vpc/                   # VPC, public/private subnets, IGW, single NAT GW
    security_groups/       # ALB SG, ECS SG, Noname SG
    ecs_cluster/           # ECS cluster, EC2 launch template, ASG, capacity provider (t3.medium)
    ecr/                   # ECR repos for kong, nginx, mulesoft plugin/wrapper images
    kong/                  # Kong OSS ECS task + ALB
    nginx/                 # NGINX OSS (OpenResty) ECS task + ALB
    mulesoft/              # Anypoint Flex Gateway ECS task + ALB; registration.yaml from Secrets Manager
    noname/                # Noname Sensor DaemonSet (host network/PID, NET_ADMIN/NET_RAW caps)

ansible/
  site.yml                 # Master playbook — runs all roles in order
  plugins.yml              # Standalone playbook — configures Noname plugin on Kong
  ansible.cfg              # aws_ec2 dynamic inventory, SSH via keys/lab_key
  vault.yml.example        # Template for secrets — copy to vault.yml, encrypt
  requirements.yml         # Galaxy: amazon.aws, community.general, community.docker
  inventory/aws_ec2.yml    # Dynamic inventory filtered by tag Project=akamai-lab
  roles/
    common/                # OS updates, system limits for container workloads
    kong/                  # Kong Admin API: creates sample service + route
    nginx/                 # Verifies NGINX ALB is reachable from localhost
    mulesoft/              # Verifies Flex Gateway ALB; accepts 200/404/502/503 (502 = no listeners pre-API-Manager-deploy)
    noname_integration/    # Registers lab-kong and lab-nginx via Noname API (lab-aws-ecs self-registers via the sensor)
    vulnerable_apps/       # Deploys VAmPI, DVGA, pixi, crAPI, Juice Shop on the apps host

docker/
  kong/Dockerfile          # FROM kong:latest + luarocks install of Noname Kong plugin; patches prevention.lua
  nginx/Dockerfile         # FROM openresty/openresty:bullseye + Noname Lua scripts; patches prevention.lua
  nginx/nginx.conf.template # nginx config with Noname Lua hooks; ${APPS_ALB_DNS} substituted at startup; resolver-based runtime DNS (variable proxy_pass + per-location rewrite, no upstream{} blocks)
  nginx/entrypoint.sh      # Runs envsubst, patches NN_SOURCE_KEY/NN_SOURCE_INDEX, starts OpenResty
  mulesoft/Dockerfile      # FROM mulesoft/flex-gateway:latest; USER root briefly to mkdir+chown conf.d for nonroot uid 65532
  mulesoft/entrypoint.sh   # Writes FLEX_REGISTRATION_YAML env var to conf.d/registration.yaml, logs diagnostics, exec /init

integration-files/         # Drop Noname-provided .zip files here (gitignored)
  noname-security-kong-policy.zip
  noname-security-nginx-policy.zip
  noname-security-mulesoft-policy.zip   # Not used — see Architecture notes

scripts/
  kong-tunnel.sh           # SSH tunnel to Kong Admin API via bastion
  traffic/locustfile.py    # Locust traffic generator — 5 FastHttpUser classes (Kong->pixi, NGINX->VAmPI, NGINX->DVGA, Kong->crAPI, Mule->Juice Shop); VAmPI/CrAPI/JuiceShop share a class-level identity pool (target 2000 authenticated users, rotated every N tasks)
  traffic/attackfile.py    # OWASP API Top 10 (2023) attack generator — 5 attacker classes, one per app; covers API1-5, API7-9 (skips API6 Sensitive Business Flows, API10 Unsafe Consumption)
  traffic/requirements.txt # locust>=2.20.0
```

## Architecture

### Network flow
Internet -> ALB (public subnets) -> ECS EC2 nodes (private subnets) -> containers. Each gateway (Kong, NGINX, Flex Gateway) gets its own ALB. The Noname Sensor runs as a DaemonSet on every cluster node with outbound-only access to the Noname engine.

### ECS approach
EC2 launch type (not Fargate) so that Ansible can SSH into the underlying nodes for configuration. Nodes run Amazon Linux 2023 ECS-optimized AMI on t3.medium. The `ecs_cluster` module manages the ASG + capacity provider; gateway modules deploy task definitions on top of it.

### Kong (dbless mode)
Kong runs in declarative (`KONG_DATABASE=off`) mode. The Ansible `kong` role configures routes/services via the Admin API (port 8001) after deployment. The Admin API port is restricted to `admin_cidr` in the SG. The Kong proxy ALB target group health check uses `path = "/"` and `matcher = "200-499"` — `/status` only exists on the Admin API port, so using it cycles tasks endlessly.

### Noname integrations and plugins
Each gateway needs both an integration registered with the engine AND a way to forward traffic. The lab uses three sources:

- **lab-kong** — Kong plugin baked into the Kong Docker image via `docker/kong/Dockerfile` (luarocks installs the rock from the zip). Task env adds `KONG_PLUGINS=bundled,nonamesecurity` when `noname_plugin_enabled=true`. `ansible/plugins.yml` fetches the correct `sourceKey`/`sourceIndex` from `GET /api/v3/sources` and pushes them via the Kong declarative `/config` endpoint (the zip ships hardcoded keys that don't match a freshly registered integration).
- **lab-nginx** — OpenResty + Lua scripts. `docker/nginx/Dockerfile` builds on `openresty/openresty:bullseye` (LuaJIT + lua-nginx-module are baked in; standard `nginx:latest` lacks them). The source key/index are injected via env var on the ECS task; `entrypoint.sh` patches them into the Lua before launching OpenResty.
- **lab-aws-ecs** — Host-level Noname Sensor DaemonSet, deployed by `terraform/modules/noname/`. Captures plaintext HTTP off each EC2 host's NIC (BPF: `tcp and not tcp port 443`). This is the only viable path for Flex Gateway visibility because the Mulesoft custom-policy zip is a Mule 4 Runtime policy and Anypoint will not apply it to Flex Gateway-served APIs ("Not covered: there is no policy implementation for the runtime version where this API is running"). Don't try the policy path again for Flex Gateway.

**prevention.lua type-guard patch**: BOTH Kong and NGINX Dockerfiles patch the Noname plugin's `prevention.lua` with a `if type(tbl) ~= "table" then return true end` guard before the `next(tbl)` call. The vendor plugin assumes `self._rules` is always a table, but when the engine returns a JSON-string error response, `cjson.decode` produces a Lua string and `next()` crashes, returning 500.

**Chicken-and-egg with images**: ECR repos must exist before images can be pushed. `make plugin-images` runs `terraform apply -target module.ecr` first, builds and pushes images, then writes `terraform/plugin.auto.tfvars` (gitignored) with the ECR URIs + `noname_plugin_enabled=true`. A subsequent `make apply` picks up the new image references.

### Noname Sensor (DaemonSet) module
- Task definition: `network_mode = "host"`, `pid_mode = "host"`, `user: root`, capabilities `NET_ADMIN, NET_RAW, SYS_NICE` (eBPF mode also adds `SYS_ADMIN, SYS_PTRACE` and host bind mounts).
- Service: `scheduling_strategy = "DAEMON"` + `launch_type = "EC2"`. One sensor per cluster instance.
- Sized 256 cpu / 512 MiB so it fits alongside the gateway tasks on each node.
- Image lives in a private Google Cloud Artifact Registry. JSON service-account key is stored in AWS Secrets Manager at `/${project_name}/noname/jfrog-credentials` (shape `{"username":"_json_key_base64","password":"<base64 GCP SA key>"}`) and referenced via the task definition's `repositoryCredentials` block.
- Source values (`noname_sniff_source_key`, `noname_sniff_source_index`, `noname_sniff_source_type`, default 201 = AWS ECS sensor) come from the Noname tenant: Settings -> Integrations -> Traffic Sources -> Add Integration -> AWS ECS tile -> Create profile -> CloudShell deployment script. **Create the source in the tenant BEFORE `terraform apply`** and copy the values into `terraform.tfvars`.
- Other tfvars: `noname_engine_url`, `noname_should_use_ebpf` (default false), `noname_jfrog_credentials_json` (sensitive), `noname_sensor_image`.

### Anypoint Flex Gateway (MuleSoft) and Juice Shop
Flex Gateway runs in connected mode. The `registration.yaml` (generated via `docker run --entrypoint flexctl mulesoft/flex-gateway registration create ...`) is stored in AWS Secrets Manager at `/${project_name}/mulesoft/registration-yaml` (12 KB, exceeds SSM Advanced tier 8KB limit). Terraform creates the secret with a placeholder and a `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [secret_string] }` block so subsequent applies do not overwrite the real value. The ECS task injects it as `FLEX_REGISTRATION_YAML`; `docker/mulesoft/entrypoint.sh` writes it to `conf.d/registration.yaml` then `exec /init`. The task execution role has `secretsmanager:GetSecretValue` on that specific ARN.

**Wrapper image plumbing — preserve these or the gateway breaks:**
- Base image runs as nonroot (uid 65532) and `/etc/mulesoft/flex-gateway/` is root-owned. The Dockerfile briefly `USER root` to `mkdir -p .../conf.d && chown -R 65532:0 .../conf.d`, then `USER 65532` before the entrypoint.
- The runtime is `/init` (a script that exports `FLEX_CONFIG_DIR=/etc/mulesoft/flex-gateway/conf.d:/usr/local/share/mulesoft/flex-gateway/conf.d` and execs `flex-agent`). `flexctl run` is not a thing — `flexctl` is just a CLI tool.
- Write target must be inside one of the `FLEX_CONFIG_DIR` paths or the agent's directory watcher won't pick it up. We use `conf.d/` (the writable entry).

**ECS service tuning:**
- `aws_ecs_service.mulesoft` sets `health_check_grace_period_seconds = 600`. In connected mode envoy has zero listeners until a proxy API is deployed from API Manager, so the gateway returns 502 on the ALB health check during startup. Without the 10-min grace window, ECS replaces the task before the agent registers with Anypoint.
- `aws_lb_target_group.mulesoft` health check uses `path = "/"`, `matcher = "200-499"`, `unhealthy_threshold = 5`. AWS rejects matchers above 499.

**Anypoint API Manager — manual one-time step (the lab does not automate it):** after Flex Gateway registers, deploy a proxy API to it from API Manager. **Implementation URI** = `<apps_alb_dns>:3000`, **Downstream Port** = `8081` (must match the ECS task's containerPort — mismatch produces 502s with no other obvious clue), **Path** = `/shop/`. Until the proxy API deploys, envoy has zero listeners, every `/shop/` request returns 502, and the `lab-aws-ecs` Sensor has no Flex Gateway traffic to capture.

### Secrets flow
- Terraform: Noname source key/index/engine URL go into `terraform.tfvars` (gitignored). Flex Gateway `registration.yaml` and JFrog/GCP SA key go into AWS Secrets Manager. SSM SecureString plumbing for tenant URL/API key is gone — the sensor reads everything from env vars baked at apply time.
- Ansible: All credentials live in `ansible/vault.yml` (gitignored, AES256-encrypted). Decrypted at runtime using `~/.vault_pass`.

## Known TODOs / Incomplete Areas

- **lab-aws-ecs Ansible registration**: The sensor self-registers, but if you want symmetry with lab-kong/lab-nginx, add a `GET /api/v3/sources` precheck in `noname_integration` for the AWS ECS source.
- **HTTPS / TLS**: ALB listeners are HTTP only. Add ACM certificate + HTTPS listeners for any scenario requiring TLS-in-transit testing.

## Implementation Notes — Do Not Regress These

- `ansible.cfg` disables ControlMaster (`ControlMaster=no`) to avoid stale SSH socket errors when going through the bastion. `pipelining = True` keeps performance acceptable.
- The common play uses `serial: 1` to avoid overloading the bastion with parallel ProxyJump connections.
- ECS AMI ships `curl-minimal` — do not install `curl` via DNF (conflict). Use the `uri` module for HTTP tasks.
- `pixi` uses `mccutchen/go-httpbin:latest` (the canonical 42crunch pixi image is not publicly available). vapi was retired and replaced by Juice Shop reached via the Flex Gateway proxy at `/shop/`.
- VAmPIUser in the locust file hits `/vampi/createdb` once per process (gated by class-level `_db_initialized` flag) — VAmPI ships with empty SQLite.
- The Makefile **must NOT** pass `--host` on the locust CLI (applies to BOTH `make traffic` and `make traffic-owasp`). It overrides per-User class hosts and routes everything to Kong (100% 404s).
- The traffic generator defaults to `TRAFFIC_USERS=50`, `TRAFFIC_RATE=5`. The attack generator defaults to `ATTACK_USERS=10`, `ATTACK_RATE=2` — keep attack cadence below baseline cadence or detection events drown in scan noise.
- Every `attackfile.py` task wraps `catch_response` and accepts `200-503` as success — attacks produce 4xx/5xx by design, locust must not count those as failures or the run aborts.
- `JuiceShopAttacker` is `abstract = True`; the concrete `_JuiceShopAttacker` only registers when `MULE_ALB_DNS` is set in env, so attack runs do not fail when Flex Gateway is absent.
- `.ansible-lint` skips `var-naming[no-role-prefix]` (cross-role shared vars) and `yaml[colons]` (visual alignment). `make lint` passes at the production profile.
- Engine ID is fetched dynamically from `GET /api/v3/engines` — no vault variable for it.
- `nginx`/`mulesoft` roles run on `localhost` and check the ALB DNS; ALB names are passed as extra-vars from Terraform output.
- No `version:` key in any Docker Compose files — obsolete in modern Docker.
- `noname_integration` does a `GET /api/v3/sources` precheck and only POSTs `lab-kong`/`lab-nginx` if the alias is missing. The Noname API does not return 409 on conflict — it just creates duplicates.
- After pulling new crAPI images that change env-var shape, wipe the postgres+mongo volumes once with `cd /opt/apps/crapi && sudo docker-compose down -v` on the apps host. Init env vars (`POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, `MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_*`) only apply to a fresh data dir.
- NGINX must re-resolve `apps_alb` at request time. `nginx.conf.template` has no `upstream {}` blocks; it sets `resolver ${DNS_RESOLVER} valid=10s ipv6=off` + `resolver_timeout 5s` at http scope, a server-scope `set $apps_alb "${APPS_ALB_DNS}"`, and uses `proxy_pass http://$apps_alb:port` (variable in URL is what forces the resolver). A static `upstream {}` or bare `proxy_pass http://hostname` resolves once at config load, caches forever, and 502s silently when AWS rotates ALB node IPs (only recovery is a force-new-deployment). Footgun: variable-based `proxy_pass` does NOT auto-strip the location prefix, so each location needs `rewrite ^/<prefix>/(.*)$ /$1 break;` plus `proxy_pass http://$apps_alb:port` (no trailing slash, no path) — without the rewrite the upstream sees `/vampi/users/v1/login` instead of `/users/v1/login` and returns 405.
- Identity pool in `locustfile.py`: `VAmPIUser`, `CrAPIUser`, and `JuiceShopUser` each hold a class-level `_identity_pool`. `on_start` seeds up to a per-class cap, capped at `USER_POOL_SIZE` (default 2000). Per-class seed defaults: `USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_VAMPI=200`, `USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_CRAPI=400`, `USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE_JUICESHOP=200`. CRAPI is highest because it has the fewest spawned instances (weight=1 vs 2 for the others) and slow signup. The classic `USER_POOL_SEED_PER_INSTANCE` env var is the fallback for VAmPI when its specific override is unset. At default settings every pool fills within ~8 min at TRAFFIC_USERS=50 (VAmPI capped <1 min, JuiceShop ~3 min, CrAPI ~8 min). Every `IDENTITY_ROTATION_INTERVAL` (default 5) tasks the instance registers a new identity if the pool is below target, otherwise picks a random existing one. `HttpBinUser`/`DVGAUser` stay stateless. JuiceShop register+login uses token path `$.authentication.token`; only `post_feedback`/`get_basket` use `self.auth`. Side effect: the apps databases accumulate ~2000 user records per stateful service over a run — only `make destroy` + redeploy resets them. Expect ~1-2 % failure rate during the seed burst (mostly transient 502s from apps under registration load).
